  <section class="prose" aria-label="Biography">
    <p class="lede">Dr. Christian Howard is a trombonist and educator based in the Twin Cities, active across orchestral, chamber, and solo settings on alto, tenor, and bass trombone and euphonium.</p>

    <h2>Teaching</h2>

    <p>Christian is Visiting Lecturer of Trombone and Euphonium at the University of Minnesota, where he leads the trombone and euphonium studio, and adjunct low brass instructor at the University of Northwestern &ndash; St. Paul. He taught Brass Methods at the University of Minnesota for three years, covering the whole brass family.</p>

    <p>His previous students have gone on to a position in a United States Navy fleet band and to study at the Eastman School of Music, Rice University, Carnegie Mellon University, and DePaul University. <a href="/teaching/">More about the studios and how to audition</a>.</p>

    <h2>Performance</h2>

    <p>Christian holds the second trombone chair with the Duluth Superior Symphony Orchestra and performs regularly as a substitute with the Minnesota Orchestra. He has reached the finals or semifinals in each of the last three years of United States Army Band &ldquo;Pershing&rsquo;s Own&rdquo; auditions, on both tenor and bass trombone, and has been a semifinalist for the United States Navy Concert Band and a finalist for The Orchestra Now.</p>

    <p>His broader performing record includes Symphony in C, LoftOpera, the Chautauqua Symphony Orchestra, the Chautauqua Music School Festival Orchestra, and the Compass Rose Brass Ensemble. He has appeared as a soloist with the University of Northwestern Symphonic Band and as a guest artist at the Texas State Trombone Symposium, and has taken part in the Third Coast Trombone Retreat, the Cleveland Trombone Seminar, and the Atlantic Brass Quintet Seminar.</p>

    <h2>Research</h2>

    <p>For his doctorate Christian transcribed three songs from Ralph Vaughan Williams&rsquo;s <i>Songs of Travel</i> for trombone &mdash; &ldquo;The Vagabond,&rdquo; &ldquo;Whither Must I Wander,&rdquo; and &ldquo;The Roadside Fire&rdquo; &mdash; alongside a study of what it takes to play a singer&rsquo;s line on a brass instrument: how the words shape the articulation, where a singer takes time, and what survives once the text is gone. The transcriptions and the paper are finished.</p>

